  const typeLessons = {
    django: ["Data types", "Django field and relationship types", "Django models use field classes to validate and store values, while relationship fields express how records connect. Field choices affect database schema, forms, admin, and query behavior.", ["Common scalar fields include CharField, TextField, IntegerField, DecimalField, BooleanField, DateField, DateTimeField, and JSONField.", "Use ForeignKey for many-to-one, OneToOneField for one-to-one, and ManyToManyField for many-to-many relationships.", "Use null, blank, choices, validators, and database constraints for distinct validation and storage needs."], "from django.db import models\n\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=120)\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=9, decimal_places=2)\n    active = models.BooleanField(default=True)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n    category = models.ForeignKey(\n        \"Category\", on_delete=models.PROTECT\n    )", "Choose a field based on domain meaning and database behavior; DecimalField is generally appropriate for exact monetary values.", "Which field represents a many-to-one relationship?", ["ForeignKey", "ManyToManyField", "BooleanField"], 0],
    fastapi: ["Types and validation", "Python types and Pydantic schema types", "FastAPI maps Python annotations to HTTP inputs and uses Pydantic models for structured validation. The type and its default together determine whether a value is required and where it comes from.", ["Use scalar types for path/query values and BaseModel fields for structured request bodies.", "Use Optional or a union with None to allow null, and provide a default to make a parameter optional.", "Use Annotated with Path, Query, Header, or Body for constraints and explicit metadata."], "from typing import Annotated\nfrom fastapi import Query\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\nclass UserIn(BaseModel):\n    name: str\n    age: int | None = None\n    tags: list[str] = []\n\nlimit: Annotated[int, Query(ge=1, le=100)] = 20", "A nullable value and an optional parameter are different: a default determines whether the caller must supply it.", "What does a Pydantic model describe in a FastAPI endpoint?", ["The shape and validation rules for structured data", "The ASGI server process type", "The database's physical storage engine"], 0],
    langchain: ["Core types", "LangChain message, document, tool, and runnable types", "LangChain components exchange structured values. Knowing the common core types makes it easier to compose models and inspect what flows through an application.", ["Messages such as HumanMessage and AIMessage carry role and content; AI messages can also carry tool calls.", "Document pairs page_content with metadata; retrievers return documents.", "Runnables define invocation and streaming interfaces; tools define named actions with schemas."], "from langchain_core.documents import Document\nfrom langchain_core.messages import HumanMessage\n\nmessage = HumanMessage(content=\"Summarize this page\")\ndocument = Document(\n    page_content=\"Source text\",\n    metadata={\"source_id\": \"guide-1\"},\n)\n\n# Model, retriever, and chain components expose Runnable behavior.", "Inspect actual message and tool-call objects when integrating providers; provider-specific content blocks can differ.", "Which LangChain type normally carries text plus source metadata?", ["Document", "BaseMessage only", "RunnableConfig"], 0],
    langgraph: ["Graph types", "State schemas, reducers, nodes, and control types", "LangGraph workflows use typed state, node functions, edges, and control values. These types define what data can flow and whether a node updates state, routes execution, or pauses it.", ["TypedDict or Pydantic schemas can describe graph state; reducers define how updates combine.", "Nodes return state updates; edges and conditional routing choose the next node.", "Command, Send, and interrupt support routing/state updates, fan-out, and human pauses."], "from typing import Annotated\nfrom typing_extensions import TypedDict\nfrom langgraph.graph.message import add_messages\n\nclass State(TypedDict):\n    messages: Annotated[list, add_messages]\n    approved: bool\n\ndef review(state: State):\n    return {\"approved\": True}  # state update", "Keep state types explicit and small; reducer semantics should match whether values replace or accumulate.", "What does a reducer specify?", ["How new state updates combine with existing state", "Which browser engine runs", "How a model token is billed"], 0],
    rag: ["RAG architecture types", "Types of RAG: architecture patterns", "There is no single universal list of mutually exclusive RAG types. A common research taxonomy describes Naive, Advanced, and Modular RAG, while GraphRAG, agentic RAG, multimodal RAG, and corrective or self-reflective RAG describe other overlapping design patterns.", ["Naive or basic RAG retrieves chunks once and generates an answer; it is a useful baseline.", "Advanced RAG adds improvements such as query rewriting, hybrid search, reranking, and context filtering. Modular RAG composes replaceable retrieval and generation stages.", "GraphRAG uses entity relationships or graph summaries for relational and global questions; agentic RAG lets a controller choose or repeat retrieval actions.", "Multimodal RAG retrieves evidence such as text, tables, images, or audio. Corrective and self-reflective patterns assess evidence or answers and may retry, search another source, or abstain."], "# Choose a pattern from the question shape\nif needs_simple_lookup(question):\n    docs = basic_retrieve(question)  # basic RAG\nelif needs_relationships(question):\n    docs = graph_retrieve(question)  # GraphRAG\nelif needs_multiple_steps(question):\n    docs = agentic_retrieve(question)  # iterative RAG\n\nanswer = generate_with_evidence(question, docs)", "These labels overlap: GraphRAG can also be modular or agentic, and advanced RAG can use multimodal sources. Choose and evaluate components for the actual task.", "Which pattern is designed to use entity relationships for multi-hop or collection-level questions?", ["GraphRAG", "Basic one-pass RAG", "Sparse retrieval by itself"], 0],
    mcp: ["Protocol data types", "MCP primitives, messages, and transport types", "MCP defines capability primitives and protocol message shapes. Distinguishing a request from a notification or result helps clients implement lifecycle, errors, and optional capabilities correctly.", ["Tools, resources, and prompts are distinct server primitives with different roles.", "Requests expect responses; notifications are one-way messages; protocol errors have structured codes and messages.", "stdio and Streamable HTTP are transport choices, not capability primitives."], "# Conceptual JSON-RPC request shape\nrequest = {\n    \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n    \"id\": 7,\n    \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n    \"params\": {\"name\": \"lookup\", \"arguments\": {}},\n}\n# A notification omits id and does not expect a response.", "Use the SDK's protocol types rather than hand-building messages for production integrations.", "Which message type normally has no request ID and expects no response?", ["Notification", "Request", "Result"], 0],
    "ai-evaluation": ["Evaluation types", "Evaluation and grader types", "Different evaluation types answer different questions. Separate tests of deterministic contracts from model-quality evaluations, and choose graders that match each criterion.", ["Offline evaluations compare a fixed dataset; online evaluation observes deployed traffic and feedback. Outcome evaluations score final answers; trajectory evaluations inspect intermediate agent actions.", "Common grader types include exact string checks, text-similarity metrics, Python/code graders, model-scored rubrics, label-model graders, and multi-graders that combine results.", "Human review adjudicates subjective or high-impact cases. Calibrate automated graders against reviewed examples and report each criterion separately."], "checks = {\n    \"schema\": python_grader(output),\n    \"similarity\": text_similarity(output, reference),\n    \"rubric\": model_grader(question, output, sources),\n    \"combined\": combine_scores(...),\n}\n\n# Keep component scores so a combined score remains explainable.", "A grader type is a measurement method, not the quality criterion itself. Define the criterion first and check that grader behavior matches it.", "Which grader is best suited to an exact schema requirement?", ["A deterministic code grader", "An uncalibrated model grader", "A live user survey only"], 0]
  };
